1
00:00:00,750 --> 00:00:02,570
We need one new label.

2
00:00:03,750 --> 00:00:07,130
Somehow, that's also a great
opportunity to accidentally change

3
00:00:07,130 --> 00:00:08,610
who can approve a request.

4
00:00:09,390 --> 00:00:10,830
Let's avoid that little adventure.

5
00:00:11,550 --> 00:00:14,030
In this lesson, we'll use Inline Edit on

6
00:00:14,030 --> 00:00:16,710
a specific piece of code, describe the behavior

7
00:00:16,710 --> 00:00:19,290
we want, and check what actually changed.

8
00:00:19,890 --> 00:00:22,210
The useful skill is setting a boundary and

9
00:00:22,210 --> 00:00:23,330
then verifying it.

10
00:00:23,890 --> 00:00:26,610
A highlighted selection helps communicate intent.

11
00:00:27,130 --> 00:00:29,090
It doesn't do your code review for you.

12
00:00:31,483 --> 00:00:33,803
We're continuing with our status helper.

13
00:00:34,643 --> 00:00:38,203
Open and approved already have
display labels, and everything

14
00:00:38,203 --> 00:00:39,823
else gets Unknown status.

15
00:00:40,563 --> 00:00:42,063
The new requirement is narrow.

16
00:00:42,603 --> 00:00:45,723
The exact value rejected should display Rejected.

17
00:00:46,543 --> 00:00:49,223
Uppercase and padded versions should remain unknown.

18
00:00:49,943 --> 00:00:52,363
This is prepared exercise code, so we can

19
00:00:52,363 --> 00:00:55,483
inspect the whole behavior without
getting lost in the application.

20
00:00:56,023 --> 00:00:57,963
We'll keep the existing branches and add the

21
00:00:57,963 --> 00:00:59,123
new case deliberately.

22
00:01:01,250 --> 00:01:02,910
Look just below the display helper.

23
00:01:03,350 --> 00:01:05,330
There's a separate function that decides whether a

24
00:01:05,330 --> 00:01:06,550
role can approve a request.

25
00:01:07,170 --> 00:01:09,150
Its policy belongs to a different decision.

26
00:01:09,430 --> 00:01:11,490
Adding a display label gives us no reason

27
00:01:11,490 --> 00:01:12,730
to change that policy.

28
00:01:13,110 --> 00:01:15,510
This neighboring function makes our boundary visible.

29
00:01:15,810 --> 00:01:17,270
The display helper may change.

30
00:01:17,850 --> 00:01:21,250
The permission helper must stay exactly as it is.

31
00:01:21,810 --> 00:01:25,730
When two responsibilities live
close together, proximity isn't

32
00:01:25,730 --> 00:01:27,070
permission to edit both.

33
00:01:29,650 --> 00:01:32,530
The prepared tests spell out the new label

34
00:01:32,530 --> 00:01:35,350
and two inputs that must remain unknown.

35
00:01:36,070 --> 00:01:38,790
Another test checks the existing permission policy.

36
00:01:39,410 --> 00:01:41,710
We still have the earlier label tests, too.

37
00:01:42,410 --> 00:01:45,490
Read those expectations before
opening the editing tool.

38
00:01:45,990 --> 00:01:49,230
Otherwise, a polished suggestion
can become the specification

39
00:01:49,230 --> 00:01:50,210
by accident.

40
00:01:51,030 --> 00:01:54,530
Here, the requirement and the checks already exist,

41
00:01:54,650 --> 00:01:57,250
so we can judge the proposed change against

42
00:01:57,250 --> 00:01:58,270
something independent.

43
00:02:01,017 --> 00:02:04,237
Run the focused tests against the starting version.

44
00:02:04,837 --> 00:02:07,477
The new label test fails because the helper

45
00:02:07,477 --> 00:02:10,317
still returns Unknown status for rejected.

46
00:02:10,897 --> 00:02:12,617
That's the gap we're here to close.

47
00:02:13,257 --> 00:02:15,177
The existing behavior checks pass.

48
00:02:15,817 --> 00:02:18,157
A failure is useful when you understand its

49
00:02:18,157 --> 00:02:20,137
cause and expect it at this stage.

50
00:02:20,837 --> 00:02:23,577
Keep the baseline result separate from the result

51
00:02:23,577 --> 00:02:26,917
after editing, otherwise you lose the evidence that

52
00:02:26,917 --> 00:02:28,957
the change addressed the missing behavior.

53
00:02:31,517 --> 00:02:33,897
Return to the source file and select the

54
00:02:33,897 --> 00:02:36,117
display helper with its contract comments.

55
00:02:36,617 --> 00:02:38,837
Leave the permission helper outside the selection.

56
00:02:39,617 --> 00:02:42,317
Now open Inline Edit with Control K on

57
00:02:42,317 --> 00:02:43,297
this Linux installation.

58
00:02:44,137 --> 00:02:46,697
Check the highlighted range
before writing the instruction.

59
00:02:47,597 --> 00:02:50,137
Including the contract comment lets the edit keep

60
00:02:50,137 --> 00:02:52,457
the explanation consistent with the implementation.

61
00:02:53,157 --> 00:02:55,757
Selecting everything would make
the intended boundary harder

62
00:02:55,757 --> 00:02:57,897
to see, even for a tiny file.

63
00:03:00,250 --> 00:03:03,210
Give the instruction three useful ingredients.

64
00:03:04,110 --> 00:03:05,430
First, the change.

65
00:03:06,130 --> 00:03:08,150
Add the exact rejected label.

66
00:03:08,870 --> 00:03:12,870
Second, what must stay true, the existing labels,

67
00:03:13,630 --> 00:03:16,730
exact matching, and the unknown result.

68
00:03:17,910 --> 00:03:19,470
Third, the exclusions.

69
00:03:20,030 --> 00:03:21,150
No normalization,

70
00:03:21,830 --> 00:03:25,050
no permission changes, and no other files.

71
00:03:25,990 --> 00:03:29,130
We also ask to keep the explicit branches.

72
00:03:29,710 --> 00:03:31,970
That's a concrete editing request.

73
00:03:32,810 --> 00:03:35,030
Notice how little depends on telling the model

74
00:03:35,030 --> 00:03:38,190
to be brilliant or to try really, really hard.

75
00:03:40,750 --> 00:03:43,710
Submit the instruction and let the edit finish.

76
00:03:44,490 --> 00:03:47,590
The amount of waiting and
the exact proposal can vary.

77
00:03:48,350 --> 00:03:51,090
Read the result before using the acceptance controls.

78
00:03:51,870 --> 00:03:54,110
Our request was for a small change in

79
00:03:54,110 --> 00:03:56,770
the selected code, but the selection isn't a

80
00:03:56,770 --> 00:04:00,370
security boundary or proof that
every constraint was obeyed.

81
00:04:00,810 --> 00:04:03,210
We'll inspect the proposed changes here, then inspect

82
00:04:03,210 --> 00:04:05,790
the actual repository diff after accepting them.

83
00:04:08,050 --> 00:04:10,690
There are two changes to inspect in this proposal.

84
00:04:11,270 --> 00:04:13,910
The contract comment names the new label, and

85
00:04:13,910 --> 00:04:16,630
a new branch returns Rejected for the exact

86
00:04:16,630 --> 00:04:17,550
rejected value.

87
00:04:18,170 --> 00:04:20,270
Check that the previous branches and the unknown

88
00:04:20,270 --> 00:04:23,130
result remain in place, then read the neighboring

89
00:04:23,130 --> 00:04:23,950
permission helper.

90
00:04:24,550 --> 00:04:27,050
An accurate comment is useful, but it can't

91
00:04:27,050 --> 00:04:28,530
compensate for incorrect code.

92
00:04:29,250 --> 00:04:32,990
The comment and implementation
should describe the same behavior.

93
00:04:35,583 --> 00:04:38,923
The visible changes match our request, so accept

94
00:04:38,923 --> 00:04:40,183
them and save the file.

95
00:04:40,803 --> 00:04:43,703
If your proposal adds extra behavior, reject it

96
00:04:43,703 --> 00:04:46,003
or refine the instruction before keeping it.

97
00:04:46,583 --> 00:04:49,703
The installed interface has
controls for reviewing changes,

98
00:04:49,983 --> 00:04:51,923
including individual changed sections.

99
00:04:52,663 --> 00:04:55,883
We're accepting this complete proposal
because we've read both parts.

100
00:04:56,563 --> 00:04:59,483
That decision keeps the edit, it doesn't create

101
00:04:59,483 --> 00:05:02,423
a Git commit, and it doesn't
replace running the tests.

102
00:05:05,050 --> 00:05:06,410
Open the Git diff.

103
00:05:06,850 --> 00:05:08,930
We want the comment update and the new

104
00:05:08,930 --> 00:05:12,030
exact branch with no unrelated file changes.

105
00:05:12,650 --> 00:05:14,750
Compare the surrounding code as well as the

106
00:05:14,750 --> 00:05:15,670
highlighted additions.

107
00:05:16,330 --> 00:05:18,230
The permission helper should be unchanged.

108
00:05:19,050 --> 00:05:21,570
This second review matters because the final working

109
00:05:21,570 --> 00:05:24,110
tree is what you'll test and eventually commit.

110
00:05:24,870 --> 00:05:27,090
Your instruction describes an intention.

111
00:05:27,550 --> 00:05:30,150
The diff describes the code you actually have.

112
00:05:32,583 --> 00:05:35,863
Run the status tests again,
then the TypeScript check.

113
00:05:36,623 --> 00:05:39,443
Now all five tests pass, including the new

114
00:05:39,443 --> 00:05:41,463
label and the permission regression check.

115
00:05:42,123 --> 00:05:44,203
Read the result and confirm that the intended

116
00:05:44,203 --> 00:05:45,163
test file ran.

117
00:05:46,003 --> 00:05:48,123
These checks support the helper change we made.

118
00:05:48,643 --> 00:05:50,623
They do not show a live user interface

119
00:05:50,623 --> 00:05:53,383
or prove the whole authorization system is correct.

120
00:05:54,023 --> 00:05:56,223
Keep the conclusion as specific as the evidence.

121
00:05:58,750 --> 00:06:02,130
In this original course exercise, choose an editing

122
00:06:02,130 --> 00:06:03,590
scope for the same request.

123
00:06:04,210 --> 00:06:06,810
The display helper and its contract comment are

124
00:06:06,810 --> 00:06:07,770
a sensible target.

125
00:06:08,410 --> 00:06:11,470
The permission helper is
unrelated, and selecting both

126
00:06:11,470 --> 00:06:13,790
functions adds unnecessary ambiguity.

127
00:06:14,550 --> 00:06:16,590
This is a teaching model, not a replacement

128
00:06:16,590 --> 00:06:17,350
for the editor.

129
00:06:18,110 --> 00:06:20,330
Use it to explain why you chose a

130
00:06:20,330 --> 00:06:23,310
range, then verify that range in your actual

131
00:06:23,310 --> 00:06:25,630
code before invoking Inline Edit.

132
00:06:28,117 --> 00:06:30,737
Now, inspect three candidate changes.

133
00:06:31,317 --> 00:06:32,997
One adds the exact label.

134
00:06:33,537 --> 00:06:36,677
Another normalizes the input, changing the result for

135
00:06:36,677 --> 00:06:38,397
uppercase and padded values.

136
00:06:39,057 --> 00:06:41,217
A third also changes permission behavior.

137
00:06:42,017 --> 00:06:43,897
Only the first matches this request.

138
00:06:44,557 --> 00:06:46,937
The other changes might
belong in separately discussed

139
00:06:46,937 --> 00:06:50,217
work, but they aren't justified
by adding a display label.

140
00:06:50,837 --> 00:06:53,537
A small change can still carry a big

141
00:06:53,537 --> 00:06:54,457
product decision.

142
00:06:54,457 --> 00:06:57,497
Read what the code means, not just how

143
00:06:57,497 --> 00:06:58,577
many lines changed.

144
00:07:01,017 --> 00:07:04,877
Record the requested change, the selected range, the

145
00:07:04,877 --> 00:07:08,017
behavior you preserved, and
the checks you actually ran.

146
00:07:09,017 --> 00:07:11,817
Include the starting revision
and the changed file set.

147
00:07:12,497 --> 00:07:14,617
Keep it short enough that a teammate would read it.

148
00:07:15,037 --> 00:07:16,257
The purpose isn't paperwork.

149
00:07:16,797 --> 00:07:18,817
It's making the decision easy to inspect.

150
00:07:19,497 --> 00:07:21,577
Here's what I asked for, here's what changed,

151
00:07:21,937 --> 00:07:24,177
and here's the evidence that the important behavior

152
00:07:24,177 --> 00:07:24,977
stayed intact.

153
00:07:27,583 --> 00:07:31,243
For your independent exercise, add one more exact

154
00:07:31,243 --> 00:07:33,863
display label from the supplied practice snapshot.

155
00:07:34,543 --> 00:07:37,783
Define its expected text and
its negative cases first.

156
00:07:38,403 --> 00:07:41,223
Use a bounded selection and a specific Inline

157
00:07:41,223 --> 00:07:42,143
Edit instruction.

158
00:07:42,643 --> 00:07:45,663
Review the proposal, inspect the final diff, and

159
00:07:45,663 --> 00:07:47,643
run the status tests and the type check.

160
00:07:48,343 --> 00:07:50,183
Leave the permission helper unchanged.

161
00:07:50,703 --> 00:07:52,783
If your model takes a different route, judge

162
00:07:52,783 --> 00:07:55,523
the behavior and explain your decision instead of

163
00:07:55,523 --> 00:07:57,343
copying our output line for line.

164
00:07:59,750 --> 00:08:02,190
You can now take a narrow requirement through

165
00:08:02,190 --> 00:08:05,130
Inline Edit without handing over the whole decision.

166
00:08:05,730 --> 00:08:08,350
Choose a range, describe the change and its

167
00:08:08,350 --> 00:08:12,570
limits, inspect the proposal,
then verify the working tree.

168
00:08:13,150 --> 00:08:15,910
In the next lesson, we'll decide when direct

169
00:08:15,910 --> 00:08:19,010
editing, Inline Edit, or Agent is the right

170
00:08:19,010 --> 00:08:20,030
tool for the job.

171
00:08:20,550 --> 00:08:23,730
Before moving on, name one behavior in your

172
00:08:23,730 --> 00:08:26,950
own code that must stay unchanged during an

173
00:08:26,950 --> 00:08:28,350
apparently simple edit.

174
00:08:28,350 --> 00:08:30,770
That's where your review should get specific.
